.\"
'\" Tcl/Tk Macro definitions - borrowed from Tcl/Tk man pages.
'\"     # Set up traps and other miscellaneous stuff for Tcl/Tk man pages.
.if t .wh -1.3i ^B
.nr ^l \n(.l
.ad b
'\" .BS Start box enclosure.  From here until next .BE, everything will be
'\"     enclosed in one large box.
.de BS
.br
.mk ^y
.nr ^b 1u
.if n .nf
.if n .ti 0
.if n \l'\\n(.lu\(ul'
.if n .fi
..
./"
'\" .BE End of box enclosure.
.de BE
.nf
.ti 0
.mk ^t
.ie n \l'\\n(^lu\(ul'
.el \{\
.\"     Draw four-sided box normally, but don't draw top of
.\"     box if the box started on an earlier page.
.ie !\\n(^b-1 \{\
\h'-1.5n'\L'|\\n(^yu-1v'\l'\\n(^lu+3n\(ul'\L'\\n(^tu+1v-\\n(^yu'\l'|0u-1.5n\(ul'
.\}
.el \}\
\h'-1.5n'\L'|\\n(^yu-1v'\h'\\n(^lu+3n'\L'\\n(^tu+1v-\\n(^yu'\l'|0u-1.5n\(ul'
.\}
.\}
.fi
.br
.nr ^b 0
..
./"
'\" .CS Begin code excerpt.
.de CS
.RS
.nf
.ta .25i .5i .75i 1i
..
./"
'\" .CE End code excerpt.
.de CE
.fi
.RE
..
./"
'\" .DS Begin an indented unfilled display.
.de DS
.RS
.nf
.sp
..
./"
'\" .DE End of indented unfilled display.
'\"
.de DE
.fi
.RE
.sp
..
./"
'\" .OP cmdName dbName dbClass
'\"     Start of description of a specific option.  cmdName gives the
'\"     option's name as specified in the class command, dbName gives
'\"     the option's name in the option database, and dbClass gives
'\"     the option's class in the option database.
.de OP
.LP
.nf
.ta 4c
Command-Line Name:      \\fB\\$1\\fR
Database Name:  \\fB\\$2\\fR
Database Class: \\fB\\$3\\fR
.fi
.IP
..
./"
'\" .SO Start of list of standard options for a Tk widget.  The
'\"     options follow on successive lines, in four columns separated
'\"     by tabs.
.de SO
.SH "STANDARD OPTIONS"
.LP
.nf
.ta 4c 8c 12c
.ft B
..
./"
'\" .SE End of list of standard options for a Tk widget.
'\"     Start of description of a specific option.  cmdName gives the
'\"     option's name as specified in the class command, dbName gives
'\"     the option's name in the option database, and dbClass gives
'\"     the option's class in the option database.
.de SE
.fi
.ft R
.LP
See the \\fBoptions\\fR manual entry for details on the standard options.
..
.\"
.\"**********************************************************************
.\"				End of Macros!!!
.\"**********************************************************************
.TH CConfigGen n 03/08/98 "CADC"
.\".BS
.SH NAME
CConfigGen 
\- Create and manipulate a CConfigGen class.

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBCConfigGen\fI \fIobjectName\fR
.\".BE

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBCConfigGen\fR command creates a new object (given by the
\fIobjectName\fR argument) and makes it into a CConfigGen object.
The \fBCConfigGen\fR command returns its
\fIobjectName\fR argument.  At the time this command is invoked,
there must not exist an object named \fIobjectName\fR.
.sp
This class behaves much like the CADC gen C library, which also
provides configuration support.

.SH "WIDGET COMMAND"
The \fBCConfigGen\fR command creates a new Tcl command whose
name is \fIobjectName\fR.  This
command may be used to invoke various
operations on the widget.  It has the following general form:
.CS
\fIobjectName method \fR?\fIarg arg ...\fR?
.CE
\fImethod\fR and the \fIarg\fRs
determine the exact behavior of the command.  
.PP
The following methods are possible for \fBCConfigGen\fR objects.
.TP
\fIobjectName \fBconfigClose\fR  
closes the configuration library.

.TP
\fIobjectName \fBconfigGet\fR \fIkey\fR \fIfunction\fR
Applys the configuration method or procedure \fIfunction\fR to 
every line in
the configuration file with keyword = \fIkey\fR.  
The first word in a line is the keyword for that line.
This function returns SUCCESS upon normal completion, 
or KEY_NOT_FOUND if the \fIkey\fR could not be found
in the configuration file.

.TP
\fIobjectName \fBconfigOpen\fR \fIappName\fR
Initializes the configuration library.  The
\fIappName\fR is used to calculate the configuration file name. The
configuration file searched for is \fIappname.config\fR where 
appname is \fIappName\fR.
.sp
This method returns a list where the first element is the
status and the second element is some string.  The status value returned
is SUCCESS upon normal completion, or ERROR if a problem occurs.
The string returned is the file name of the configuration file,
upon normal completion, and an error description if an error
occurs.
.sp
The configuration file is located by the
environement variable APPNAME_CONFIG_DIR  directory, where
APPNAME is \fIappName\fR.
If environment variable APPNAME_CONFIG_DIR
is not set, then the directory defined by environment
variable DEFAULT_CONFIG_DIR is searched.  Finally, if the
configuration file is not located from either of these two
environment variables, the current directory is searched.


.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gen_config (3)

.SH EXAMPLE
.DS
 proc displayTokens {numTokens tokens} {
     set output ""
     for {set idx 1} {$idx < $numTokens} {incr idx} {
         append output [lindex $tokens $idx]
     }
     puts "TOKENS: $output"
 }
 CConfigGen myConfig 
 myConfig configClose	
 myConfig configGet {token1 displayTokens}
 myConfig configGet {token2 displayTokens}
 myConfig configOpen myApplication
.DE

.SH AUTHOR
Shannon Jaeger

.SH KEYWORDS
gen library, configuration file, object
